==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*(s)ker- (turn)}}
From {{inh|en|enm|schrynken}}, from {{inh|en|ang|sċrincan}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*skrinkwaną}}. Cognate with {{cog|nl|schrinken||to shrink}}.

The sense {{m-g|psychologist, psychotherapist}} is a {{clipping|en|head-shrinker|nocap=1}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈʃɹɪŋk/}}
* {{audio|en|En-au-shrink.ogg|a=AU}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪŋk|s=1}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|shrinks|shrinking|shrank|past2=shrunk|past2_qual=nonstandard|shrunk|past_ptc2=shrunken}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To cause to become [[smaller]].
#: {{ux|en|The dryer '''shrank''' my sweater.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2008|month=October|author=David Schipper|title=Outsmart your stomach: Seven ways to fill your gut—and lose it, too|journal=w:Men's Health|volume=23|issue=8|issn=1054-4836|page={{gbooks|VMgDAAAAMBAJ|135|shrink}}|passage=The bottom line is this: To '''shrink''' your gut, you need to start listening to it.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To become smaller; to [[contract]].
#: {{ux|en|This garment will '''shrink''' when [[wet]].}}
#* {{RQ:Bacon Sylva Sylvarum|passage=I have not found that water, by mixture of ashes, will '''shrink''' or draw into less room.}}
#* {{RQ:Dryden Annus Mirabilis|passage=And '''shrink''' like parchment in consuming fire.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2018|url=https://learningenglish.voanews.com/a/china-s-melting-glacier-brings-visitors-concern-over-climate-change/4627742.html|title=VOA Learning English > China's Melting Glacier Brings Visitors, Adds to Climate Concerns
|passage=Since 1982, it has '''shrunk''' by 250 meters. [[File:Since 1982, it has shrunk by 250 meters.ogg]]}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2021 October 6|author=Greg Morse|title=A need for speed and the drive for 125|journal=RAIL|issue=941|page=52|text=When they took over the 1000 'Flying Scotsman' from May 1979, the journey from London to Edinburgh '''shrank''' to just 4hrs 37mins - including a stop at Newcastle.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[cower]] or [[flinch]].
#: {{ux|en|Molly '''shrank''' away from the blows of the whip.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To draw back; to withdraw.
#* {{RQ:Milton Poems|poem=Nativity|page=10|passage=The Libyc ''Hammon'' '''ſhrinks''' his horn,}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|figuratively}} To withdraw or retire, as from danger.
#* {{RQ:Pope Essay on Man|passage=What happier natures '''shrink''' at with affright, / The hard inhabitant contends is right.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1881|translator=w:Benjamin Jowett|title=Thucydides
|passage=They assisted us against the Thebans when you '''shrank''' from the task.}}
#* {{RQ:Heller Catch-22|The Soldier in White|169|The other patients in the ward, all but the Texan, '''shrank''' from him with a tenderhearted aversion from the moment they set eyes on him the morning after the night he had been sneaked in.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To move back or away, especially because of fear or disgust.

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|avoid an unwanted task}} {{l|en|funk}}, {{l|en|shirk}}
* {{sense|withdraw or retire, as from danger}} {{l|en|shrink back}}, {{l|en|retreat}}

====Antonyms====
* {{antsense|to cause to become smaller}} {{l|en|expand}}, {{l|en|grow}}, {{l|en|enlarge}}, {{l|en|stretch}}
* {{antsense|become smaller}} {{l|en|expand}}, {{l|en|grow}}, {{l|en|enlarge}}, {{l|en|stretch}}

