===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# [[shrinkage|Shrinkage]]; [[contraction]]; [[recoil]].
#* {{quote-book|en |author=w:Leigh Hunt |chapter=To T** L** H**, Six Years Old, During a Sickness. |title=Foliage;{{nb...|or Poems Original and Translated}} |page=xlvii |pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=93MkAAAAMAAJ&pg=RA1-PR47 |year=1818 |location=London |publisher=Printed for C. and J. Ollier, Welbeck Street |text=Yet almost with, with sudden '''shrink''', That I had less to praise. }}
# {{lb|en|slang|sometimes|pejorative}} A [[psychiatrist]] or [[psychotherapist]].
#: {{syn|en|head-shrinker|shrinker}}
#: {{cot|en|shrinkette}}
#: {{ux|en|You need to see a '''shrink''', you crazy fool.}}
#: {{ux|en|My '''shrink''' said that he was an enabler, bad for me.}}
#* {{quote-song|en |year=1994 |month=August |author=w:Green Day |title=[[w:Basket Case (song)|Basket Case]] |album=w:Dookie |publisher=w:Reprise Records |track=7 |text=I went to a '''shrink''' to analyze my dreams / She says it's lack of sex that's bringing me down}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2021 March 10|title=Stop & Examine|journal=RAIL|issue=926|page=70|text="From behind the counter of this provincial train station coffee shop, Joanna was barista and unofficial '''shrink''' to wildly varied London-bound travellers," writes author Laline Paull. ''Confessions of a Barista on Platform 1'' was published on February 9 by The Firle Press [...].}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable|business}} Loss of [[inventory]], for example due to [[shoplifting]] or not selling items before their [[expiration  date]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2011|author=Charles Sennewald; John Christman|title=Retail Crime, Security, and Loss Prevention: An Encyclopedic Reference|page=227
|passage=Assuming the retailer's '''shrink''' is average or below, and the owner is comfortable with the level of '''shrink''', perhaps nothing more need be done except to maintain vigilance and to monitor the '''shrink''' for signs of emerging problems.}}

====Usage notes====
* {{sense|therapist}} The slang sense was originally pejorative, expressing a distrust of practitioners in the field. It is now not as belittling or trivializing.

